some of Hollywood's biggest stars are  celebrating a landmark decision by the  Supreme Court on Monday the Supreme  Court ruled that it is illegal for an  employer to fire someone because of  their sexual orientation or gender  identity the six to three ruling adds  LGBTQ people to title seven of the Civil  Rights Act which already protected  against discrimination based on sex race  color national origin and religion  justice Neel Gorsuch wrote an employer  who fires an individual merely for being  gay or transgender defies the law many  celebs were quick to celebrate the news  on social media I'm Ellen and I'm gay  Ellen DeGeneres tweeted about the  historic moment  Kerry Washington shared a bunch of  colorful heart emojis and my big LGBT  advocate anytime I can show my support  it's you know I will and I'll show up  it's awesome Meghan McCain wrote that  the ruling was huge and long overdue and  it's really great to see Cynthia Nixon  said it was a huge victory for LGBTQ  rights and Mandy Moore wrote that the  news is a bright spot in a dark time  it's exciting Busy Philipps Jonathan Ben  s George Takei Clay Aiken Hillary  Clinton Anthony Rapp and Candis Cayne  also shared their excitement and in a  series of tweets Justin Makita wrote  about the landmark win for LGBTQ  equality adding quote I love the idea  that someday all will be treated with  equal dignity and respect tennis legend  Billie Jean King tweeted that Monday's  decision was a huge win for the LGBTQ  community the Supreme Court ruled that  living authentically is not a fireable  offense Janet mock tweeted that the  ruling is a victory hard-won in the  courts and on the streets  I'm deeply hopeful by the work that  everyday people are taking to challenge  conversations at their own dinner tables  et caught up with Janet last week and  she addressed the transgender community  explaining that the fight for equality  starts with your inner circle  exactly where you are at your kitchen  table with your family your community  your chosen loved ones that you know you  have those conversations there and then  there you build more and more and more  to be an activist or to be a  co-conspirator and an accomplice almost  yeah yes an accomplice exactly right you  know we have to be active we have to  mean distant and we have to do the  uncomfortable work of checking and  challenging conversations glad president  and CEO Sarah Kate Ellis also responded  to the ruling telling ET in a statement  quote the Supreme Court's historic  decision affirms what shouldn't have  even been a debate LGBTQ Americans  should be able to work without fear of  losing jobs because of who they are the  decision gives us hope that as a country  we can unite for the common good and  continue the fight for LGBTQ acceptance  [Music]
 

